Intellectual pleasure — the joy of understanding, of solving a problem, of connecting ideas — is a form of pleasure that deepens with more knowledge, whereas sensory pleasures plateau or habituate.
Mill's 'competent judges' — those who have experienced both higher and lower pleasures — consistently prefer intellectual engagement, suggesting this preference is not mere snobbery but reflects something real about comparative value.
Knowledge enables autonomy: an informed person can evaluate their own situation, understand their options, and make genuine self-determining choices in a way that an uninformed person cannot.
